<!-- §01 The Quality Ceiling -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Quality Ceiling</div>
<h2>The Speculative Decoding Bottleneck</h2>
<p>Speculative decoding is one of the most successful techniques for accelerating large language model inference. The core idea is simple: a cheap <em>draft model</em> proposes γ tokens in sequence, and the expensive <em>target model</em> verifies all γ in a single parallel pass. If most drafts are accepted, you spend near-zero extra compute on verification while generating multiple tokens per step.</p>
<p>The catch: the drafter needs to be <strong>fast</strong> (to keep drafting overhead low) and <strong>accurate</strong> (to keep the accept rate high). These two requirements pull in opposite directions — making the drafter larger improves quality but raises latency, eating into the speedup. EAGLE-3, the state-of-the-art autoregressive drafter, resolves this with a single-layer transformer that predicts future tokens one at a time, conditioned on the target's hidden states. Empirically, it achieves roughly 2.2–2.7× speedups.</p>

<div class="callout amber">
  <div class="callout-title">The AR drafter's wall</div>
  <p>Autoregressive drafters generate tokens <strong>sequentially</strong> — each token depends on the one before. Even a 1-layer drafter must make γ sequential passes to propose γ tokens. Drafting cost scales linearly: Tdraft = γ × t<sub>step</sub>. Making it deeper raises t<sub>step</sub>; making it wider raises t<sub>step</sub>. There is no free lunch.</p>
</div>

<p>The visualization below contrasts the two drafting paradigms. The key insight is that an autoregressive drafter's quality is fundamentally limited by the capacity it can afford at low latency.</p>

