<p>The right side of this diagram is the key: a block diffusion drafter generates <em>all</em> γ tokens in one parallel forward pass. Its cost is roughly constant regardless of γ. This is DFlash's secret — and it opens a door that AR drafters keep closed.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 DFlash -->
<section id="s-dflash" data-code="dflash">
<div class="section-no">02 — DFlash: Parallel Drafting</div>
<h2>DFlash: The Target Knows Best</h2>
<p>DFlash (Block Diffusion for Flash Speculative Decoding) makes one elegant observation: large autoregressive target models already encode multi-token future context in their hidden states. The draft model doesn't need to reason from scratch — it just needs to efficiently "read" the target's plan and fill in the masked tokens.</p>
<p>The architecture has two key ingredients: <strong>fused context features</strong> (extracted from the target's last layer) and <strong>bidirectional attention</strong> in the drafter (since all γ token positions can see each other in a single denoising pass). This is fundamentally different from EAGLE-3, which generates tokens one at a time in a causal fashion.</p>

<div class="step-list" id="dflash-steps">
  <div class="step-box active">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Extract Target Features</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Run the target LLM's last transformer layer. The hidden state h<sub>t</sub> ∈ ℝ<sup>d</sup> at each position implicitly encodes future-token distributions — even for tokens not yet generated.</div>
      <div class="step-code">h = target.forward(context)  # full hidden state</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Mask the Draft Block</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Allocate a block of γ masked positions: x = [MASK, MASK, …, MASK]. The diffusion drafter will denoise all γ positions simultaneously, conditioned on h.</div>
      <div class="step-code">x = [MASK] * gamma  # γ draft positions</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">One-Shot Block Diffusion</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The drafter performs a single denoising step: using bidirectional attention over all γ positions plus context from h, it predicts token distributions for every position in parallel.</div>
      <div class="step-code">draft = drafter.denoise(x, context=h)  # parallel!</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Target Verification</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The target LLM verifies all γ proposed tokens in one parallel forward pass. Accepted tokens are kept; the first rejected token triggers a resample and a new draft cycle begins.</div>
      <div class="step-code">accepted = target.verify(draft, context)  # all γ in one pass</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="df-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <button id="df-next">Next →</button>
  <span id="df-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Step 1 of 4</span>
</div>

<p>The result is striking. On Qwen3-8B, DFlash achieves <strong>6.08×</strong> speedup on GSM8K and <strong>5.51×</strong> on HumanEval — compared to EAGLE-3's 2.75× and 2.17× on the same tasks. The key reason: a 5-layer DFlash drafter generates 16 tokens in roughly the same latency as a 1-layer EAGLE-3 drafter generating a single token, yet with far higher accept rates thanks to the target's conditioning.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">DFlash Speedup Formula</div>
  speedup = <span class="math-var">τ</span> / (<span class="math-var">T<sub>draft</sub></span> + <span class="math-var">T<sub>verify</sub></span>) × <span class="math-var">T<sub>target</sub></span><br>
  where <span class="math-var">T<sub>draft</sub></span> = <span class="math-var">t<sub>parallel</sub></span> ≪ γ × <span class="math-var">t<sub>step</sub></span>   <span class="math-comment">← parallel, not sequential</span><br>
  and <span class="math-var">τ</span> = expected accepted tokens per cycle (including bonus token)
</div>

<p>But here's what DFlash doesn't yet exploit: the drafter makes exactly <strong>one</strong> denoising pass. It has no mechanism to refine its predictions — to "think again" about uncertain positions before committing. That's where LoopMDM comes in.</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 LoopMDM -->
<section id="s-looping" data-code="looping">
<div class="section-no">03 — LoopMDM: Free Depth</div>
<h2>LoopMDM: Loops Give Free Depth</h2>
<p>LoopMDM (arXiv:2605.26106) introduces a simple architectural change: instead of running each transformer layer once, selectively loop a small shared block of early-middle layers S times. The parameter count stays the same; the effective depth becomes nh + S·nm + no. Loop count S is the key variable.</p>
<p>What makes this work in masked diffusion models? The key intuition is that <strong>masked positions act as a shared workspace</strong>. In a single denoising step, the model must jointly predict all masked tokens while respecting global consistency constraints. One pass through the transformer isn't enough — the model makes locally plausible but globally inconsistent predictions. Repeating the shared block lets masked positions "talk to each other" across multiple iterations, progressively refining inconsistencies.</p>

<div class="callout cyan">
  <div class="callout-title">Sudoku: a concrete test</div>
  <p>LoopMDM was tested on 9×9 Sudoku with a <strong>forced left-to-right generation order</strong> (same as autoregressive) — a notoriously hard setting because early cells are predicted without full context. With S=1, the model makes 24 errors. With S=2, only 7. With S=3: 0 errors. The loop count directly controls how many times masked positions can revise their inconsistent predictions.</p>
</div>

<!-- Sudoku visualization -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">sudoku solver — errors vs loop count S</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click S to change loop count</span>
  </div>
