<p>Why is this especially promising for speculative decoding? Two reasons:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Drafting cost stays parallel.</strong> Adding S loops multiplies T<sub>draft</sub> by S, but it's still one forward pass over γ positions (not γ sequential passes). GPU parallelism means T<sub>mid</sub> is very small compared to T<sub>target</sub>.</li>
  <li><strong>Better drafts = fewer rejected tokens.</strong> A 13% perplexity improvement in the draft model translates directly to a higher accept rate τ, which is in the numerator of the speedup formula. More accepted tokens per cycle means more effective acceleration.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout green">
  <div class="callout-title">Why this works for speculative decoding specifically</div>
  <p>LoopMDM's gains are most pronounced on <em>globally constrained tasks</em> — Sudoku, math reasoning, code. These are exactly the workloads where speculative decoding sees the most benefit (long CoT sequences). The looped drafter helps most where the draft model currently struggles most.</p>
</div>

<p>Critically, no new parameters are needed. The shared mid-block is trained with stochastic loop counts S ∼ U&#123;1, …, S<sub>max</sub>&#125;, making it robust to any S at inference time. You can tune S post-deployment to match the latency budget of each serving environment.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 Trade-off -->
<section id="s-tradeoff" data-code="tradeoff">
<div class="section-no">05 — S vs Speedup</div>
<h2>Finding the Sweet Spot: Loop Count vs Net Speedup</h2>
<p>The key question is: how does net speedup change as we increase S? More loops → better draft quality → higher accept rate τ. But more loops also increase T<sub>draft</sub> = S × T<sub>mid</sub>. There must be an optimal S.</p>
<p>To reason about this, we combine DFlash's speedup formula with LoopMDM's measured quality improvements. The chart below shows estimated curves — using DFlash's reported latency (T<sub>parallel</sub> ≈ T<sub>target</sub>/9 for 16 draft tokens on Qwen3-8B) and LoopMDM's measured accept rate proxies (generative perplexity improvements at each S).</p>

<!-- Interactive canvas: S vs speedup -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">loop count S vs net speedup (projected)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover to read values</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="tradeoff-canvas" style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:580px;margin:0 auto" height="260"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted);text-align:center;margin-top:.5rem;margin-bottom:0">
      Projected estimates based on DFlash latency figures and LoopMDM perplexity improvements. Actual results require empirical measurement.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The projected curve peaks at around <strong>S=3–4</strong>. Beyond that, the extra drafting cost outpaces the quality gain. This matches an important asymmetry: accept rate improvements diminish as S grows (a 5% improvement in generation perplexity from S=6 to S=12), while drafting cost grows linearly.</p>

<h3>The speedup formula with looping</h3>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Projected Speedup — Looped DFlash</div>
  speedup(S) = <span class="math-var">τ(S)</span> / (<span class="math-var">S · T<sub>mid</sub></span> + <span class="math-var">T<sub>verify</sub></span>) × <span class="math-var">T<sub>target</sub></span><br>
  where <span class="math-var">τ(S)</span> ≈ τ₀ · (1 + δ(S))   <span class="math-comment">← accept rate improves with S</span><br>
  and <span class="math-var">δ(S)</span> ≈ Δ·PPL<sub>gain</sub>(S) / PPL<sub>0</sub>   <span class="math-comment">← from LoopMDM perplexity data</span>
</div>

<p>At S=1 (baseline DFlash), the formula reduces to the original DFlash speedup. The additional S loops add S × T<sub>mid</sub> to the denominator but also improve τ in the numerator. For moderate S (2–4), the numerator gain dominates. For large S (≥8), the denominator catches up.</p>
</section>

<!-- §06 Adaptive Looping -->
<section id="s-adaptive" data-code="adaptive">
<div class="section-no">06 — Adaptive Looping</div>
<h2>Adaptive Looping: Peak Quality at Half the Cost</h2>
<p>LoopMDM reports one more crucial finding: loops are not equally useful at all positions. The NLL improvement from additional loops <strong>peaks at intermediate denoising timesteps</strong> — when the model has enough context to reason about constraints, but enough unresolved positions to benefit from revision. Early timesteps (nearly all masked) and late timesteps (nearly all revealed) benefit less.</p>
<p>This observation motivates a simple adaptive strategy: monitor the relative hidden-state change between successive loop iterations,</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Early Exit Criterion</div>
  δ<sub>k</sub> = ‖H<sup>(k)</sup> − H<sup>(k−1)</sup>‖ / ‖H<sup>(k)</sup>‖   &lt; ε   →   stop looping
</div>
<p>When δ<sub>k</sub> falls below a threshold ε=0.1, the hidden states have converged — additional loops add no value. LoopMDM showed this reduces the average loop count from S=12 to roughly <strong>S̄=5.1</strong> while preserving nearly all the performance gains (zero-shot PPL: 51.7→52.9, downstream accuracy: 41.4→41.3).</p>

