<h3>Why adaptive looping matters for speculative decoding</h3>
<p>In a speculative decoding loop, not every draft round is equally hard. When the context is highly predictable (e.g., continuing a well-defined code function), the drafter converges in 2–3 loops. When the context is ambiguous (e.g., an open-ended reasoning branch), more loops help. Adaptive looping naturally allocates compute where it matters.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">The combined picture</div>
  <p>A looped DFlash drafter trained with S<sub>max</sub>=12 and deployed with adaptive early exit (avg S̄≈5) gets the best of three worlds: DFlash's parallel drafting speed, LoopMDM's free depth for quality, and adaptive looping's compute efficiency. The expected speedup profile: near-peak speedup at a fraction of the full-loop cost.</p>
</div>

