<p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted)">* Projected estimate based on combining DFlash's latency model with LoopMDM's measured quality improvements. Not empirically validated — presented as a research hypothesis.</p>

<p>The core insight is structural: DFlash has already solved the <em>parallelism</em> problem in drafting. LoopMDM addresses the <em>quality</em> problem in parallel decoding. These two techniques are designed for exactly the same class of architectures (masked diffusion transformers) and compose naturally. The loop count S becomes a simple knob to tune the quality–latency trade-off post-deployment.</p>

<p>The most interesting open question is whether the LoopMDM training recipe (stochastic S sampling, selective early-middle layer looping) transfers cleanly to the DFlash training setup, where the drafter is also conditioned on target hidden states. We expect the target conditioning makes this <em>easier</em> — the draft model doesn't need to reason entirely from scratch, reducing the number of loops needed to converge.</p>
</section>

